## Record real changes

Keep the first publication date stable. Add the newest substantive update first and explain its practical effect. Use ISO dates in `<time datetime>` and a readable British date in visible text. Do not replace all dates on each build or imply that unchanged content has been reviewed.

For existing pages, this rebuild retains the first publication date found in repository history and records the actual documentation-shell change. New pages declare their dates and notes in frontmatter. The layout refuses to build a guide without a history record. Future editors must append an accurate entry when guidance or its examples materially change.

## Expandable history behaviour

Use a native `details` element with “See all updates” as its summary. The ordered list is newest first. Enter and Space toggle the disclosure; its state is available without custom JavaScript. Keep the metadata visible and make the history readable when printed.

## Publishing-component provenance

The GOV.UK publishing library merged Published dates into Metadata in [PR #5572](https://github.com/alphagov/govuk_publishing_components/pull/5572), released in 66.9.0. The current full-history parameter is `page_history`, with `first_published` and `last_updated`; `see_updates_link` supports the upper-page link. The old standalone component was subsequently removed. The simple `history` field is not the full update array, and `show_history` is not the option used by the current source.

This Middleman site implements the same editorial pattern with semantic HTML. It does not install the Rails publishing gem. Its own frontmatter names are `published_on`, `updated_on` and `updates`, mapped by the shared layout.

## Testing and content ownership

Check a new page, a page with several changes, long notes, keyboard operation and no JavaScript. The displayed dates must match the authored record. A build date, dependency release or Git commit time alone does not establish a content review. Keep ownership and source links near the guidance, and use the repository issue route to report inaccuracies.
